00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:58.940
OK, so firstly, a
look at the ecosystem.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:01.040
You never hear
mention of Android

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.606
without mention
of the ecosystem,

00:04:02.606 --> 00:04:03.730
perhaps in the same breath.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:06.950
The two are pretty
much intertwined.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:09.590
Here are some properties that
I find particularly exciting

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:13.790
and particularly interesting
about Android as an ecosystem.

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:16.339
First is devices.

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:19.100
There is so much choice
of different devices

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.290
that you can put to
work for your users

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:23.390
and for your
particular use case.

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:26.310
You'll hear a lot about
the power of the cloud,

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:28.760
particularly in this
conference, and the tools that

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.720
are available there
to really serve

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.270
a lot of different large
compute requirements.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:37.610
I think, actually, we
have one of the best

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.310
jobs within this ecosystem,
which is actually

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.220
getting to play with all
these physical devices

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.030
and making sure that--

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.420
helping our partners really
innovate and make sure

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.640
that there's a device
available to meet

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.807
pretty much any different
use case you can imagine.

00:04:51.807 --> 00:04:53.390
There's a few different
verticals here

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:55.264
that are almost conspicuous
by their absence.

00:04:55.264 --> 00:04:58.160
I've pulled out the
most traditional devices

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.230
and their appeal for
enterprise management,

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:02.170
but obviously conspicuous
by their absence

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:07.730
are whole verticals in auto
internet of things, obviously,

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.456
and TV.

00:05:09.456 --> 00:05:10.580
So there's definitely more.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:11.954
And the point that
I want to make

00:05:11.954 --> 00:05:15.860
is that the diversity here is
definitely more than just price

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:16.550
point.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:19.910
Its about having a device for
any particular different use

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:21.440
case that you have in mind.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:24.380
Whether that's [INAUDIBLE]
a single purpose

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.660
deployment that you heard
my colleague, Vidia, talking

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:27.950
about earlier.

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:31.100
Whether its traditional
smartphone use case.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:34.780
Whether its signage
or kiosk purposes.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:39.410
There really is a device for
everything you can imagine.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:42.950
Now with the scale and with
the diversity of the Android

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:47.750
ecosystem, it's easy to imagine
that some amount of complexity

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:51.200
may come with deploying
applications and managing

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.500
devices at this kind of scale.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:56.510
And while that's certainly true,
the way that I look at this

00:05:56.510 --> 00:06:01.010
is that Android provides this
platform, this underlying

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:04.269
layer of consistency,
where otherwise you

00:06:04.269 --> 00:06:05.310
wouldn't be able to have.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:09.020
For example, your same set of
developers write the same app

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:12.066
and have it run in a
consistent way across most

00:06:12.066 --> 00:06:13.190
of these different devices.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:15.170
They would have, but
wouldn't, perhaps,

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:20.150
be able to benefit from
the unified deployment

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:21.620
platform in Google Play.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:23.750
And so there's a lot
that we do and is really

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.300
front of mind for the
Android team, in general.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.160
And certainly, for
the enterprise team,

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.810
one of the areas
that we invest in

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:31.580
is ensuring that
these devices can

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:34.520
be deployed in a
consistent way to our users

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:36.912
and managed using a
consistent API surface.

00:06:36.912 --> 00:06:38.870
So that's something
incredibly important to us.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:40.828
I won't stand in front
of you today and pretend

00:06:40.828 --> 00:06:43.310
that it's job done, there,
certainly a work in progress,

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:45.220
but that's something
that we find powerful

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:48.580
and hopefully you do to.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:51.160
The final point that
I'll note is obviously

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:53.020
these devices are
capable of connecting

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:54.850
to so many different
cloud backends

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:57.880
that I'm sure you'll hear more
about later on in the talk.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:01.450
But just from a raw
compute power perspective,

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:04.510
these devices are getting
so much more capable day in,

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:06.430
day out, that
certainly the flagship

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:10.600
smartphones that you come across
today are incredibly capable.

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:13.480
The software that runs
on them, the apps,

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:16.010
is only getting more and
more powerful over time.

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:19.300
And we're starting
to see these replace

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:23.470
a lot of general purpose desktop
kind of use cases already.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:26.350
This is something that the
team is very excited about

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:30.920
and we see growing a
great deal over time.

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:34.147
OK, so a few more big
numbers, apologies.

00:07:34.147 --> 00:07:35.480
This is about the Play platform.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:38.440
It will be kind of remiss
to talk about the largest

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:40.360
mobile ecosystem in the
world without talking

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:41.890
about the largest
app distribution

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:44.920
platform in the world, which
is of course, Google Play.

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:48.070
Some of these numbers are
just astounding, I find.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:50.110
65 billion-plus app
installs for Play.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:51.610
I believe that was
in the past year.

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:55.300
A million-plus apps
available, unique

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:58.810
apps within the store to
serve all of the different use

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:01.450
cases you can imagine for
these different devices.

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:04.240
And a billion-plus 30-day
active users of Google apps.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.070
That's Google apps
with a lower case a.

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:09.250
That's apps provided by Google.

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:12.160
And obviously, in the app
side of the ecosystem, the app

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:13.930
distribution side,
I'll talk a bit more

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:17.410
about this like what we
build into the Play platform

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:22.030
to help you manage these
deployments at scale, shortly,

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:23.530
but this is a key
area, again, where

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:28.660
we strive to bring
consistency to the ecosystem.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:31.160
And finally, in
this set of slides,

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:33.400
a quick nod to all
of our partners who

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:34.669
make this possible.

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:37.870
Obviously this isn't the full
gamut of partners for Android

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:41.643
as an ecosystem,
but it's phenomenal,

00:08:41.643 --> 00:08:43.059
and again, quite
humbling to think

00:08:43.059 --> 00:08:46.960
that a lot of these
businesses, if not totally

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:49.675
put a great deal of--

00:08:49.675 --> 00:08:53.830
are able to benefit a great
deal from the ecosystem at large

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:57.910
and provide for a great deal
of their business from it.

00:08:57.910 --> 00:08:59.530
There's a partner
for almost any use

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:02.170
case in any deployment you
can imagine or set of partners

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:03.400
to come and enable that.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:06.550
So we're immensely grateful
to our partners, some of which

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:07.750
I know are here today.

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:12.840
OK, so I don't need
to spend too much time

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.520
talking about the size and
scale of Android, perhaps.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:20.250
Onto what we do and what
my team works on in order

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:22.920
to help you secure
these devices and get up

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:26.360
and running as
quickly as possible.

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:28.150
And again, the goal
that we focus on here,

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:30.690
first and foremost, is to
bring as much consistency

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:33.210
to the management and the
deployment of these devices as

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:35.970
possible, and be able
for your employees

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:38.490
to reach into their pocket
and pull out an Android phone,

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:41.285
and get them up and running
and productive in seconds.

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:47.780
So firstly, let's take a look
at what the platform provides.

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:50.582
How does it all fit together?

00:09:50.582 --> 00:09:52.040
Really, on this
slide, the platform

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:55.050
is everything in the lower
right side of the diagram.

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:56.810
Everything that's
outlined in blue

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:01.170
here is stuff that the wider
enterprise team focuses on.

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:04.090
It's top of mind for us
and we provide toolsets

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:07.520
in each of these components to
help you get up and running.

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:10.340
From the platform perspective,
obviously the bottom block here

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:14.280
you see is the Android Open
Source Project, AOSP for short.

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:16.610
This is the really powerful
bit of the platform

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:19.610
that enables all of our
partners to extend and submit

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:23.240
contributions to Android,
an what really makes

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:25.010
it unique as a mobile platform.

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:27.770
Most of the stuff that
our team actually works on

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:31.959
goes into this AOSP
level of the platform.

00:10:31.959 --> 00:10:33.500
That's the management
modes that I'll

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:37.310
talk through later-- the
provisioning, the policies that

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:38.510
get enforced.

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:41.360
Most of it goes into this
core underlying layer

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:43.010
that underpins everything else.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:44.780
Immediately above
the AOSP layer,

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:49.020
you have Google Mobile
Services, or GMS for short.

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:50.540
And these provide
really everything

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:53.042
that is Google about
Android devices.

00:10:53.042 --> 00:10:55.500
Some notable components that
you all have heard of in there

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:57.120
is Google Play itself.

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.684
There's a suite of security
components in there

00:10:59.684 --> 00:11:01.100
that I'll talk a
little bit about,

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:03.230
and you'll hear more
about in some other talks

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:07.690
throughout the conference.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:10.280
And so, these two
pieces as a whole

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:11.980
provide the core platform.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:15.500
There's an API surface that's
exposed through these two

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:19.460
components, and we work with
our EMM partners, our Enterprise

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:22.700
Mobility Management partners,
who actually sit at the app

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:24.720
level just above this.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:28.940
The EMMs provide an app which
is able to call these APIs.

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:31.610
They're really responsible
for setting up the devices

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:35.870
and choosing which policies
to enforce to really cater

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:39.500
for your particular deployment
or your particular vertical,

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:41.360
shaping that platform
into something

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:45.500
that's able to serve those use
cases as smoothly as possible.

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:48.940
Immediately above that we
have, again on the app side,

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:51.110
there's a suite of apps
that Google provide,

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:54.020
most notably Gmail,
Calendar, et cetera.

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:57.050
We as an enterprise team
put a huge deal of effort

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:00.040
into actually making sure
that the likes of Gmail

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:03.350
is able to get up and
running on whatever

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:04.690
email backend you may have.

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:08.030
If your company is using
Microsoft Exchange ActiveSync

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:11.480
as a stack, for example,
it's very important to us

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:14.990
that you are able to get up and
running on that using Gmail,

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:17.240
and we put a lot of
effort into having

00:12:17.240 --> 00:12:19.580
your users be able
to get out of the box

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:23.480
and productive by default.

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:26.470
Likewise, the enterprise apps--

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:29.540
I highlighted the
million-plus different apps

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:33.260
available in Play.

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:36.620
This is a huge amount of
investment in diversity

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:38.690
that comes from the
ecosystem and is really

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:40.760
made possible from
our ecosystem partners

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:43.075
to serve all of these
different use cases.

00:12:43.075 --> 00:12:44.450
Last but not least
on this slide,

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:47.750
we have a set of services
that the team invests in.

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:49.430
This is, most
notably, Google Play.

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:53.147
There's a suite of APIs that is
available to our EMM partners

00:12:53.147 --> 00:12:54.230
to manage and deploy apps.

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:57.290
I'll talk a little bit about
how that function shortly.

00:12:57.290 --> 00:13:00.500
And safety net, which is sort of
the broad term for the security

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:04.310
components that are bundled
by default in GMS Android

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:07.730
devices, which again, I'll
drill into a little bit

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:09.660
in an upcoming slide.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:12.350
So here's a quick look at
where Android management has

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:15.860
come from and I'll finish up
by having a quick look at where

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:17.700
we're going.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.330
So the first thing
to note, probably,

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:21.830
is that we've rebranded.

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:24.950
You probably won't see much
mention of Android for Work

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:25.830
anymore.

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:29.180
This was a rebrand, or
technically an unbrand,

00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:30.800
back in December of last year.

00:13:33.590 --> 00:13:36.260
And what's happened, how
the platform has evolved,

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:38.570
has really led to all
of these tools that

00:13:38.570 --> 00:13:41.060
were previously known of
as being part of Android

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:43.200
for Work, as being
part of that offering.

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:46.810
They're now completely bundled
into any Android device.

00:13:46.810 --> 00:13:49.190
So any Android device
that you pick up

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:51.390
will be capable of running--

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:53.000
a work-managed
device, for example,

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:56.420
will be capable of supporting
a profile as long as it's not

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:59.600
a low RAM device,
for example, and is

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:02.820
capable of deploying all
this uniform platform

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:04.160
policies throughout.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:08.125
So the unbrand, we think, makes
a lot of sense in that respect.

00:14:08.125 --> 00:14:10.250
It does make it slightly
difficult to actually talk

00:14:10.250 --> 00:14:11.930
about what we as a team do.

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:15.500
So I'm thinking of starting a
nice little sideline of Android

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:19.250
for Work swear jar and I
think that will generate

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:20.870
a lot of profit,
if not just for me,

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:24.590
during the course of
this presentation.

00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:25.160
OK.

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:31.140
So more about the team's
work and what we're up to.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:34.260
So the first management
mode, if you like,

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:38.100
and the first capability
that we invested in way back

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:40.960
in Lollipop when we came
to produce what I'll

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:43.740
refer to as sort of the
modern set of management APIs

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:46.190
that are available,
is the work profile.

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:52.830
The work profile really allows
you to keep your employees'

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:55.030
data separate from--

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:56.850
keep your work data,
rather, separate

00:14:56.850 --> 00:14:59.310
from your personal data
on these deployments.

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:01.380
We think that this is a
really powerful building

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:05.500
block for BYOD in particular.

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:09.960
So for example, just in order
to sync work data to the device

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:12.120
in order to be productive
as an end user who

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:16.170
owns their own device,
it's easy to imagine

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:19.380
that a lot of work data needs
to be synced, but also a lot

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:21.240
of what policy needs
to come with that data

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:23.340
to keep it secure.

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:27.330
And the work profile
really contains that data,

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:29.040
and the management
that comes with it,

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:30.570
and ensures that
nothing spills out

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:32.740
into the personal
side of the device.

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:35.490
So we find this incredibly
powerful for both end

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:39.030
users, who no longer need to
worry about management taking

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:41.260
over the rest of their
device, being able to wipe out

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:44.940
their personal apps and
photos or anything else,

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:48.660
and also for IT admins who don't
want the burden of thinking

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:50.970
about this aspect
of their deployment,

00:15:50.970 --> 00:15:53.400
and thinking about
how users are going

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:55.200
to have to hand over
that much control

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.785
over their personal devices.

00:15:57.785 --> 00:15:59.910
So some notable aspects of
the work profile and the

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:02.150
features that it provides.

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:04.710
If for whatever reason your
work data needs to be wiped,

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:08.580
most notably the profile
just disappears, the profile

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:11.230
and the work data
that goes with it.

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:14.100
You can have a separate
set of policies

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:17.490
that enforce the screen lock
complexity to get into the work

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:18.420
apps.

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:22.320
We find this is incredibly
powerful for most.

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:23.340
So family use cases.

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:26.310
You can give your device
to a friend or family

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:29.280
without worry about the
work data that's accessible

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:32.020
or on that device.

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:33.570
And they're
independently lockable.

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:36.180
The user is in
full control here.

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:38.280
So this is one of the
two management modes

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:40.500
here that we've put
in from Lollipop

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:42.210
and is available
in the platform.

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:46.260
The other is targeted for
corporate-owned devices.

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:48.090
So this is explicitly
anything that

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:50.350
is not owned by an end user.

00:16:50.350 --> 00:16:52.740
This has a few different names.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:55.800
Device Owner Mode is the
name of the API surface

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.510
that underpins everything here.

00:16:57.510 --> 00:16:59.010
You'll hear me talk
about it as just

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:01.680
in the context of
corporate-owned devices a lot

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:03.360
as well.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:07.020
And really the focus with
corporate-owned devices

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.930
is striving for maximum
flexibility and maximum

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:12.420
capability in terms
of the management

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:15.060
that you want to place on this
device, in terms of the use

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:17.450
cases that you
want to set it to.

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:20.310
And so you saw in
the devices slide

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:24.119
a while back the breadth
of different devices

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:26.490
that are available.

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:28.349
This mode really
caters for anything

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:32.520
from knowledge work on
a corporate owned device

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:36.975
all the way to fixed-purpose,
hotel check-in kiosks,

00:17:36.975 --> 00:17:38.610
or special purpose devices.

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:41.910
So a very broad range
of functionality.

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:44.990
For example, notably you can
reset data on the whole device.

00:17:44.990 --> 00:17:47.930
You can enforce policies that
affect Wi-Fi and the Bluetooth

00:17:47.930 --> 00:17:50.370
stack, and so on and so forth.

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:56.145
You have a limited amount
of control of OTAs,

00:17:56.145 --> 00:17:57.020
Over The Air updates.

00:17:57.020 --> 00:17:59.228
When they hit the device
you can change a maintenance

00:17:59.228 --> 00:18:00.920
window, which is
important for some

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:02.660
of our [INAUDIBLE]
use cases, to ensure

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:05.090
that your signage
situation stays up

00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:08.710
and running when you want it to.

00:18:08.710 --> 00:18:09.460
OK.

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:12.180
So next up in this
section, what do we

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:15.290
do as a team for Google Play?

00:18:15.290 --> 00:18:17.550
And I think this is a
really powerful component

00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:20.760
of the overall picture here.

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:22.020
Firstly, a little rebrand.

00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:23.186
It used to be Play for Work.

00:18:23.186 --> 00:18:25.110
Now it's Managed Google Play.

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:27.550
Some more contributions
for the Android for Works

00:18:27.550 --> 00:18:29.820
swear jar, no doubt.

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:36.070
So this is another area where
we simplify and, we think, bring

00:18:36.070 --> 00:18:39.930
a big investment in terms of
a common layer of management

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:42.180
and ability to
update apps common

00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:43.720
throughout the ecosystem.

00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:45.690
So the first thing
to note here is

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:49.080
that public apps, any of
those million-plus apps

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:50.580
that I talked to
you about earlier,

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:55.560
can be selected and curated as
an IT admin for your enterprise

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:58.710
deployments and for your users.

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:00.930
Those apps, once you've
selected and created

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:03.390
what's necessary
for your deployment,

00:19:03.390 --> 00:19:07.080
you can use in conjunction with
your EMM partner and the Play

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:08.370
Management Console.

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:10.470
You can have those
apps pushed out

00:19:10.470 --> 00:19:14.430
to your end user devices, push
installed inside a profile,

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:19.570
inside the workspace, or on
the managed device as a whole.

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:22.650
And you can have these
devices just up and running,

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:24.870
by default, as soon as--

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:27.090
the end user only
needs to set up

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:29.340
the basic level of
management on the device,

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:31.290
and the rest of that
deployment experience

00:19:31.290 --> 00:19:33.310
can be taken care of for them.

00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:37.020
And we find that
incredibly powerful.

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:40.110
Also, in terms of
facilities and feature

00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:44.040
set from the Play
side of the stack,

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:47.790
you can have these
apps also updated.

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:49.290
You can do that
with private apps

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:51.570
just as well as you can
do it with public apps.

00:19:51.570 --> 00:19:54.130
So if your use case requires,
for whatever reason,

00:19:54.130 --> 00:19:58.260
apps that aren't available to
the public or even self-hosted,

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:00.030
privately hosted,
then again, you

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:04.500
can do this and use Play as this
unified stack for distribution.

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:07.440
Only the metadata, for example,
in the privately hosted app use

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:10.320
case needs to be
submitted to Play.

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:13.800
The last, and I find most
exciting bit of the puzzle

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:16.380
here, is configuration
at the app level.

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:18.810
So we have what's called
a Mobile Configuration

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:22.080
Management, or MCM for short.

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:24.780
And that allows these
enterprise applications

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:30.180
to take configuration through
the DPC, through the EMM,

00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:31.734
and through the Play stack.

00:20:31.734 --> 00:20:33.150
Wherever's most
convenient for you

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:35.140
to store that configuration.

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:37.350
And so this really
pieces together

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:40.070
to form a very coherent
and slick end-to-end

00:20:40.070 --> 00:20:41.820
set-up experience, we find.

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:44.170
And so when your users
sign up and enroll,

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:48.150
then you'll not only have
the right apps to get up

00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:52.140
and running and be productive
by default, those apps that also

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:55.230
contain a weird and wonderful
mix of different server

00:20:55.230 --> 00:20:58.200
addresses, and ports,
and certificate names,

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:01.260
and whatever else, you name
it, configuration to get up

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:04.350
and running in your
enterprise environment, that

00:21:04.350 --> 00:21:08.950
can all be supplied, by default,
to the apps through the stack.

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:12.030
So all the user needs to
enter is perhaps a password.

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:15.240
You can even leverage some
of our native apps, SSO work,

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:18.360
and maybe they don't always need
to enter their password either.

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:21.646
So out-of-box
productivity and speed

00:21:21.646 --> 00:21:23.520
of deployment for the
end users is definitely

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:27.190
something that's top
of mind on our side.

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:30.150
Another thing that's top of
mind on our side is security.

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:33.090
So I'll just talk a
little bit about security

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:34.464
in this presentation.

00:21:34.464 --> 00:21:36.880
I'd highly recommend, if you
didn't catch it this morning,

00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:40.380
Adrian Ludwig, he's the
director of security on Android.

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:43.080
I highly recommend you
watch that talk on YouTube

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:44.280
when it becomes available.

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:46.920
And likewise, I'll plug
another talk tomorrow

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:48.780
with Edward
Cunningham, where he'll

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:51.390
dive into a lot
more detail about

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:54.930
what's available in the
Android security suite and how

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:56.950
that all fits together,
how that works.

00:21:56.950 --> 00:21:59.820
So I highly recommend those two.

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:03.750
I'll just start by talking
a little bit about the scale

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:04.590
here.

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:12.690
This is from our upcoming
security year in review, 2016.

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:15.090
And the numbers are,
again, pretty large.

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:16.830
A billion-plus
devices protected.

00:22:16.830 --> 00:22:20.600
This is out of the
box for GMS Android.

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:24.510
400 million device scans per
day and six billion-plus apps

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:25.890
checked per day.

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:27.930
So we noted in our
previous year in review,

00:22:27.930 --> 00:22:29.490
that that probably
makes us the--

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:31.560
well, we believe
we are the largest

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:34.260
provider of endpoint
protection in the world

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:35.089
with these figures.

00:22:35.089 --> 00:22:36.630
We're certainly the
least well known.

00:22:39.330 --> 00:22:41.420
In terms of what that
means in numbers,

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:45.600
again, so these are the
rights of Potentially Harmful

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:48.030
Applications, or PHAs.

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:50.389
Again, much more detail
in the security sessions

00:22:50.389 --> 00:22:52.680
about how those break down
in the different categories,

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:54.638
but essentially, for the
purposes of this talk,

00:22:54.638 --> 00:22:57.240
we can consider them malware.

00:22:57.240 --> 00:23:00.120
The first thing to note is
that the malware incidence

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:04.650
rates, the blue line here
for all sources of apps--

00:23:04.650 --> 00:23:06.600
this is including
side-loaded apps,

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:08.850
and apps that come from
multiple different app stores,

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:11.220
and multiple geographies--
the incidence rates

00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:13.380
are fairly low and declining.

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:14.900
This is the blue bar.

00:23:14.900 --> 00:23:18.270
And even those other sources
of apps-- if you side

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:20.040
load an app on
Android, then you'll

00:23:20.040 --> 00:23:23.490
still be protected by default
by something called Verify App.

00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:25.877
So those apps are still
going to get scanned,

00:23:25.877 --> 00:23:27.960
and still going to get
checked, and you will still

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:31.140
have prompts to uninstall, and
warnings for known bad behavior

00:23:31.140 --> 00:23:32.310
in those apps.

00:23:32.310 --> 00:23:34.380
So you see this number
decreasing, even

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:35.350
on the top line.

00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:37.170
The bottom line,
again, probably more

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:39.900
relevant for a lot of
enterprise deployments,

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:42.870
is incidents rates from
using Google Play only

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:45.890
for your app distribution,
which are an order of magnitude

00:23:45.890 --> 00:23:46.390
lower.

00:23:49.891 --> 00:23:50.390
OK.

00:23:50.390 --> 00:23:52.199
So onto some of
the best practices.

00:23:52.199 --> 00:23:53.990
We have talked a little
bit about the tools

00:23:53.990 --> 00:23:55.610
that we provide,
some of the features

00:23:55.610 --> 00:23:57.770
that we provide in
Android as a platform,

00:23:57.770 --> 00:24:00.770
how can you put them to use in
your customers' organizations

00:24:00.770 --> 00:24:03.560
or in your own organizations.

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:06.650
This is quite a broad topic,
as you may have guessed.

00:24:06.650 --> 00:24:09.590
So what I'm going to do is try
and sketch out some of the axes

00:24:09.590 --> 00:24:11.500
by which we look at
these management modes,

00:24:11.500 --> 00:24:14.480
and which you may be able to
imagine some of the deployments

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:17.330
that you're familiar with, and
how they fall on these axes.

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:24.170
So firstly, here you see the
horizontal axis that I've

00:24:24.170 --> 00:24:27.290
drawn here is the
use of the device,

00:24:27.290 --> 00:24:31.250
and the vertical axis is,
broadly speaking, the value.

00:24:31.250 --> 00:24:33.770
And we go clockwise
round in terms

00:24:33.770 --> 00:24:36.740
of the level of management
that is required

00:24:36.740 --> 00:24:38.660
for this particular deployment.

00:24:38.660 --> 00:24:41.230
In the bottom right
here, you have

00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:45.390
what's basically a traditional
feature phone kind of segment.

00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:47.870
There's little work data
synced to the device,

00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:50.930
and hence, little management
and need for management.

00:24:50.930 --> 00:24:52.940
This is an area
that I mentioned.

00:24:52.940 --> 00:24:56.180
We want to make sure that users
benefit from the productivity

00:24:56.180 --> 00:25:00.170
side, even if they don't need
the full heavyweight management

00:25:00.170 --> 00:25:01.940
that comes with it.

00:25:01.940 --> 00:25:04.470
This is where the traditional
stack starts to come in.

00:25:04.470 --> 00:25:09.110
So the top of this
chart here, you

00:25:09.110 --> 00:25:12.980
see the traditional knowledge
worker type use cases,

00:25:12.980 --> 00:25:16.490
and that just ranges from
personal devices and the work

00:25:16.490 --> 00:25:19.340
profile, all the way
through to managed

00:25:19.340 --> 00:25:24.830
devices and our corporate-owned
device owner, API feature set.

00:25:24.830 --> 00:25:27.890
And finally, everything on
the left-hand side of this

00:25:27.890 --> 00:25:28.790
diagram--

00:25:28.790 --> 00:25:32.480
this is all catered for with
our broad range of device owner

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:34.160
APIs, this whole vertical.

00:25:34.160 --> 00:25:36.430
So anything that
is corporate-owned,

00:25:36.430 --> 00:25:40.760
that is not personally
owned explicitly, this API

00:25:40.760 --> 00:25:42.710
surface caters for
knowledge work all the way

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:45.530
down to your
fixed-purpose kiosks

00:25:45.530 --> 00:25:47.780
that you may have heard about
in the previous session.

00:25:50.860 --> 00:25:54.600
So that was a little bit
about how the API surfaces fit

00:25:54.600 --> 00:25:57.960
together and how they can be
deployed within enterprise

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:00.180
that you may be familiar with.

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:01.830
A little bit of some general--

00:26:01.830 --> 00:26:03.840
I'll have a look at some
general best practices

00:26:03.840 --> 00:26:07.620
and then I'll talk
about pulling those out

00:26:07.620 --> 00:26:12.360
in terms of who owns the device
on your particular use case.

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:14.160
So no surprises here.

00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:17.990
You heard about the
instance rates of PHAs

00:26:17.990 --> 00:26:19.290
when installing from Play.

00:26:19.290 --> 00:26:22.320
We recommend that if your
deployment doesn't require it

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:25.050
as a hard requirement, that
you disable unknown sources.

00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:29.970
You place all of your apps
within an apps distribution

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:34.620
channel that you trust and that
is useful for your deployment.

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:35.650
Device encryption.

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:37.380
So if you caught
Adrian's talk, you'll

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:39.720
have seen the statistics
and the prevalence

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:42.480
of device encryption, and
how it's increased over time.

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:47.280
I think it was 80%, he finished
up with, back in version

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:51.510
7 in Nougat of devices
now having encryption

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:54.330
enabled by default. So this
may not be top of mind any more

00:26:54.330 --> 00:26:56.163
when you're thinking
about your deployments.

00:26:56.163 --> 00:26:59.100
We recommend, nonetheless,
that you can enforce a policy

00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:02.720
to require it, and we have
a surface of policies that

00:27:02.720 --> 00:27:05.730
is able to do that for you.

00:27:05.730 --> 00:27:07.020
Minimum security updates.

00:27:07.020 --> 00:27:08.910
So again, the
security talk, this

00:27:08.910 --> 00:27:12.030
is something that's really top
of mind for the security team.

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:15.840
There are policies that are
available to require and set

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:19.210
a minimum age, in terms of the
previous security update that

00:27:19.210 --> 00:27:21.840
hit your particular device
that you're managing,

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:23.760
and we recommend
that you'll consider

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:25.590
enabling those for your fleet.

00:27:25.590 --> 00:27:29.180
I'll talk a little bit more
about those in the next slide.

00:27:29.180 --> 00:27:31.220
Excuse me.

00:27:31.220 --> 00:27:34.840
We recommend that
when at all possible,

00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:36.260
if appropriate for
deployment, you

00:27:36.260 --> 00:27:39.410
would want to keep
your work data

00:27:39.410 --> 00:27:43.220
and your personal data of your
employees on those devices

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:46.970
separate and managed separately.

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:50.750
Last but not least here, use
the latest management APIs

00:27:50.750 --> 00:27:52.670
that are available with
a particular device.

00:27:52.670 --> 00:27:56.270
So I mentioned earlier we have
what we call the modern managed

00:27:56.270 --> 00:28:00.320
set of Management APIs that
came about with Lollipop Plus.

00:28:00.320 --> 00:28:02.900
There aren't many
devices now that you'll

00:28:02.900 --> 00:28:05.330
encounter in your average
enterprise deployments that

00:28:05.330 --> 00:28:07.240
don't support those.

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:11.840
But we recommend instead of
treating the older device

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:15.480
admin API surface as, perhaps,
a least common denominator,

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:18.590
we recommend that you support
the latest and greatest when

00:28:18.590 --> 00:28:20.510
it's available.

00:28:20.510 --> 00:28:22.422
And if necessary,
for older devices,

00:28:22.422 --> 00:28:23.880
if they're part of
your deployment,

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:25.504
if they're part of
your fleet, then you

00:28:25.504 --> 00:28:29.360
can fall back gracefully
to that older API surface,

00:28:29.360 --> 00:28:33.080
and perhaps bring with
it less privileged access

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:35.270
to less sensitive
information if that's

00:28:35.270 --> 00:28:37.014
appropriate for your deployment.

00:28:37.014 --> 00:28:38.930
And again, you can work
with your EMM partners

00:28:38.930 --> 00:28:42.560
to decide where
that bar should be.

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:45.290
In terms of deployment
type, here I've

00:28:45.290 --> 00:28:47.435
split it out in terms
of personally owned

00:28:47.435 --> 00:28:49.290
and corporate-owned devices.

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:50.540
So again, no surprises here.

00:28:50.540 --> 00:28:52.860
The management mode that
you should be looking at,

00:28:52.860 --> 00:28:55.300
if you're looking at a
BYOD deployment, profile

00:28:55.300 --> 00:28:56.750
owner versus device owner.

00:28:56.750 --> 00:28:58.910
Security patches,
this is slightly

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:02.665
different in terms of your BYOD
versus corporate-owned devices.

00:29:02.665 --> 00:29:04.040
So on the BYOD
side, you're going

00:29:04.040 --> 00:29:08.940
to get a different range
of different devices taken

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:12.360
and activated within
your environment.

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:16.880
So what you can do here is
A, look at the security patch

00:29:16.880 --> 00:29:19.760
level that's available
on this device.

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:23.120
Set, perhaps, a
cutoff or threshold

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:28.660
by which the age of the device
doesn't meet your requirements,

00:29:28.660 --> 00:29:30.920
or that the security
patch level will not

00:29:30.920 --> 00:29:33.170
give access to the most
sensitive resources.

00:29:33.170 --> 00:29:36.900
And this is how we manage
devices at scale within Google,

00:29:36.900 --> 00:29:39.700
for example.

00:29:39.700 --> 00:29:42.300
On the corporate-owned
side, we recommend

00:29:42.300 --> 00:29:46.020
that you look at OEMs who
are regularly shipping

00:29:46.020 --> 00:29:47.595
these monthly security patches.

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:52.550
If this is important
for your deployment,

00:29:52.550 --> 00:29:55.500
favor those devices.

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:59.010
For UX, we really look at this
through the lens of device

00:29:59.010 --> 00:30:02.130
set-up and getting users up and
running as quickly as possible,

00:30:02.130 --> 00:30:05.340
taking them to that value
as quickly as possible.

00:30:05.340 --> 00:30:08.290
Turns out no users go to set-up
management on their devices.

00:30:08.290 --> 00:30:10.110
Who knew?

00:30:10.110 --> 00:30:11.610
They just go to
check their email

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:13.651
and get kind of frustrated
by the management that

00:30:13.651 --> 00:30:15.160
gets in the way of it.

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:17.580
And so, this is something
that the team thinks

00:30:17.580 --> 00:30:20.940
very hard about,
and we invest a lot

00:30:20.940 --> 00:30:24.189
in minimizing the steps
required to get up

00:30:24.189 --> 00:30:25.230
and running in the space.

00:30:25.230 --> 00:30:28.590
We recommend that you will
enforce policies and work

00:30:28.590 --> 00:30:31.500
with your EMM partners and your
app vendors to do the same,

00:30:31.500 --> 00:30:33.990
and really take away any
hurdles, particularly

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:36.600
in the BYOD space, in the
personally owned devices,

00:30:36.600 --> 00:30:39.570
to get the users up and
running extremely quickly.

00:30:39.570 --> 00:30:41.250
Privacy.

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:43.560
We think with the work
profile API surface

00:30:43.560 --> 00:30:45.450
that I mentioned
earlier, we think

00:30:45.450 --> 00:30:48.600
we've set what we consider
a good baseline in terms

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:49.380
of privacy.

00:30:49.380 --> 00:30:52.230
You have these two things,
these two worlds separated

00:30:52.230 --> 00:30:54.660
by default, and there's
very little management

00:30:54.660 --> 00:30:57.450
that is able to spill
outside of the work profile.

00:30:57.450 --> 00:30:59.010
So we recommend
that that baseline

00:30:59.010 --> 00:31:02.190
will be hopefully suitable
for your BYOD deployments.

00:31:02.190 --> 00:31:04.230
We recommend you just
keep your users fully

00:31:04.230 --> 00:31:07.240
informed about anything
that could, in fact, impact

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:07.920
that privacy.

00:31:07.920 --> 00:31:09.750
Likewise on corporate
owned devices,

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:11.700
I'll take you
through some things

00:31:11.700 --> 00:31:14.040
that we're working
on there to help

00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:17.580
you inform your users
of any management that

00:31:17.580 --> 00:31:19.991
may impact privacy.

00:31:19.991 --> 00:31:20.490
OK.

00:31:20.490 --> 00:31:22.920
So what is the team
hard at work on?

00:31:22.920 --> 00:31:23.870
What's coming up next?

00:31:23.870 --> 00:31:25.703
It's kind of the namesake
of the conference,

00:31:25.703 --> 00:31:29.070
so I thought I'd better slip
in a few bits of information

00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:33.990
about what you can expect in a
future release of the platform.

00:31:33.990 --> 00:31:37.560
First and foremost, here
we have work profiles

00:31:37.560 --> 00:31:40.140
are going to be available
for corporate devices.

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:43.380
So this is almost a merging
of the two worlds here.

00:31:43.380 --> 00:31:47.300
What we found is that
the separation that

00:31:47.300 --> 00:31:51.210
is enabled by work profiles
is immensely popular,

00:31:51.210 --> 00:31:54.630
and it's immensely popular
not just in the BYOD space,

00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:57.540
but also in corporate
deployments.

00:31:57.540 --> 00:31:59.730
And these corporate
deployments don't

00:31:59.730 --> 00:32:03.450
want to have full oversight
over the personal side

00:32:03.450 --> 00:32:05.720
of the device,
over personal data,

00:32:05.720 --> 00:32:07.980
but they do want
to allow the work

00:32:07.980 --> 00:32:10.680
data to exist in
a separate space

00:32:10.680 --> 00:32:14.460
and benefit from all of these
policies that we make possible.

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:17.640
You can define exactly
how data crosses these two

00:32:17.640 --> 00:32:20.830
boundaries as an IT admin.

00:32:20.830 --> 00:32:22.870
And we find this
really can improve

00:32:22.870 --> 00:32:29.510
the usability that's possible
in corporate device deployments.

00:32:29.510 --> 00:32:33.080
Closely related to a previous
feature and my note on privacy,

00:32:33.080 --> 00:32:37.070
something that's upcoming
here is disclosures, reminders

00:32:37.070 --> 00:32:41.360
if you like, of the fact that
A, the device is managed,

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:44.320
B, the name of the organization
that is managing this device

00:32:44.320 --> 00:32:48.590
is not just on the lock screen
you see in this screenshot,

00:32:48.590 --> 00:32:52.100
but a number of prevalent places
to remind the user that they're

00:32:52.100 --> 00:32:54.710
not using a personal
unmanaged device.

00:32:54.710 --> 00:32:56.600
They are using a
corporate device here.

00:32:56.600 --> 00:32:59.120
And the second feature, the
second part of this feature,

00:32:59.120 --> 00:33:02.600
is what we call the privacy hub,
here, on the left-hand side.

00:33:02.600 --> 00:33:05.180
And this really
discloses any policies

00:33:05.180 --> 00:33:07.370
that are in effect on the
device at this given point

00:33:07.370 --> 00:33:11.630
or that could be put
in effect, and what

00:33:11.630 --> 00:33:13.010
this device is capable of.

00:33:13.010 --> 00:33:15.080
Some of the more
privacy-impacting actions--

00:33:15.080 --> 00:33:19.700
for example, taking a bug
report, sharing a security log.

00:33:19.700 --> 00:33:21.920
You can see when those
were last carried out

00:33:21.920 --> 00:33:23.132
on this particular device.

00:33:23.132 --> 00:33:24.590
And again, the goal
here is to help

00:33:24.590 --> 00:33:28.160
you keep users fully informed
so that they can make the best

00:33:28.160 --> 00:33:32.040
decisions about their privacy.

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:32.990
Device provisioning.

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:35.815
I mentioned this is a huge
area of focus within the team,

00:33:35.815 --> 00:33:37.440
and it's something
we've invested a lot

00:33:37.440 --> 00:33:40.954
in on the upcoming release.

00:33:40.954 --> 00:33:42.120
A huge amount of investment.

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:46.800
In fact, this effort, here,
is focused on the BYOD side

00:33:46.800 --> 00:33:48.250
of the spectrum.

00:33:48.250 --> 00:33:51.120
So this is the work
profile set-up flow.

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:54.420
We really re-examined all of
the features and the components

00:33:54.420 --> 00:33:57.690
that go into the work profile
set-up flow from the ground up.

00:33:57.690 --> 00:33:59.430
It turns out it's
enormously complex

00:33:59.430 --> 00:34:04.590
to have this secondary user
created on the Android device,

00:34:04.590 --> 00:34:06.200
have the profile populated.

00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:09.179
There's a lot of
server-side flows involved

00:34:09.179 --> 00:34:12.730
in the set up of your average
profile deployment as well.

00:34:12.730 --> 00:34:14.940
So we've reexamined all
of those dependencies, all

00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:16.620
of those components,
and we've managed

00:34:16.620 --> 00:34:19.585
to increase the speed of set
up by a factor of approximately

00:34:19.585 --> 00:34:20.085
10x.

00:34:23.909 --> 00:34:26.010
In fact, the maximum
here, the ceiling

00:34:26.010 --> 00:34:29.940
is about 15 seconds, maximum,
on the most complex of our setup

00:34:29.940 --> 00:34:31.050
flows.

00:34:31.050 --> 00:34:35.520
The goal here is to get the
user to that value as soon

00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:36.780
as possible.

00:34:36.780 --> 00:34:39.300
What we've also
done and realized

00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:43.290
as we've iterated on
the work profile concept

00:34:43.290 --> 00:34:45.750
is that there's
really very little

00:34:45.750 --> 00:34:48.300
to be scared of as an end
user in setting this up.

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:49.810
This is a benefit to the user.

00:34:49.810 --> 00:34:51.750
It's a benefit to the admin.

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:54.600
And so what was previously
present in the flow

00:34:54.600 --> 00:34:56.550
is a lot of different parties.

00:34:56.550 --> 00:34:58.860
There's a lot of
different components

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:00.704
that are involved in
managing a device,

00:35:00.704 --> 00:35:02.370
and each of those
parties tended to have

00:35:02.370 --> 00:35:04.990
their own terms of service,
their own disclosures.

00:35:04.990 --> 00:35:08.070
And that would inevitably lead
to a bit of a wall of text

00:35:08.070 --> 00:35:10.742
and a wall of scariness,
for want of a better word,

00:35:10.742 --> 00:35:13.200
that users were faced with when
they were setting these up.

00:35:13.200 --> 00:35:16.500
What we've offered here
is an extensible surface

00:35:16.500 --> 00:35:19.890
that you can use to populate
these terms and disclosures.

00:35:19.890 --> 00:35:23.100
And so if the user's interested,
they can tap into View Terms

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:25.800
here and they'll see a
collapsible list of everything

00:35:25.800 --> 00:35:27.990
that applies to their
usage of the profile.

00:35:27.990 --> 00:35:30.430
That can come from your
organization, your company,

00:35:30.430 --> 00:35:33.090
via the EMM, come from
the EMM itself, certainly,

00:35:33.090 --> 00:35:36.510
from Google, and from parts
of the platform, and the OEM.

00:35:36.510 --> 00:35:38.310
So all of those can
be consolidated now

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:42.144
into one screen.

00:35:42.144 --> 00:35:43.560
So really,
provisioning is just as

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:46.170
simple on the platform
side as one screen

00:35:46.170 --> 00:35:48.000
that you accept and continue.

00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:51.330
So this is something
we're very excited about.

00:35:51.330 --> 00:35:54.840
Other improvements
on the work profile.

00:35:54.840 --> 00:35:57.840
So really, the goal
that we've set ourselves

00:35:57.840 --> 00:36:00.990
here is to help admins
to really double down

00:36:00.990 --> 00:36:05.160
on this API surface,
and look at areas where

00:36:05.160 --> 00:36:07.900
management sometimes needs to
spill outside of the profile.

00:36:07.900 --> 00:36:10.200
I mentioned that we
try to minimize that as

00:36:10.200 --> 00:36:11.229
much as possible.

00:36:11.229 --> 00:36:13.020
And the default answer
to anything spilling

00:36:13.020 --> 00:36:15.660
outside the profile
is always no.

00:36:15.660 --> 00:36:17.430
But there are some
legitimate cases

00:36:17.430 --> 00:36:20.640
where you want to help ensure
the integrity of the rest

00:36:20.640 --> 00:36:26.010
of the device, and so we're
putting some features in here

00:36:26.010 --> 00:36:28.410
to really help with that
particular use case.

00:36:28.410 --> 00:36:31.950
But still not impacting that
personal usage of the device

00:36:31.950 --> 00:36:33.840
to the extent possible.

00:36:33.840 --> 00:36:36.300
What we looked at here
and what we are launching

00:36:36.300 --> 00:36:37.750
is Verify App strict mode.

00:36:37.750 --> 00:36:41.020
So here, any warnings that were
present as part of the Verify

00:36:41.020 --> 00:36:44.610
Apps process that
scans apps on install,

00:36:44.610 --> 00:36:46.320
they can become
blocking, and they

00:36:46.320 --> 00:36:48.450
can prevent potentially
harmful apps

00:36:48.450 --> 00:36:50.800
from being installed on the
personal side of the device.

00:36:50.800 --> 00:36:54.660
Likewise, we're coming out with
a lot of new APIs within Verify

00:36:54.660 --> 00:36:58.740
Apps to inspect the integrity
and the status of any apps

00:36:58.740 --> 00:37:01.680
that are installed within the
managed part of the device.

00:37:04.470 --> 00:37:06.530
OK, data at risk protection.

00:37:06.530 --> 00:37:09.260
So something I mentioned
earlier that's really

00:37:09.260 --> 00:37:12.050
become prevalent and commonplace
throughout the ecosystem

00:37:12.050 --> 00:37:15.300
recently is encryption.

00:37:15.300 --> 00:37:17.540
So devices are now
benefiting from encryption

00:37:17.540 --> 00:37:19.640
by default. They're
also benefiting,

00:37:19.640 --> 00:37:21.350
as of the last
release, from what we

00:37:21.350 --> 00:37:23.940
call file-based
encryption by default,

00:37:23.940 --> 00:37:27.590
which is a lot more flexible
system for encrypting

00:37:27.590 --> 00:37:28.680
these devices.

00:37:28.680 --> 00:37:31.400
It allows, for example,
the device to boot up

00:37:31.400 --> 00:37:35.480
and for apps to be what
we call encryption aware,

00:37:35.480 --> 00:37:39.110
and for those apps to expose
whatever functionality

00:37:39.110 --> 00:37:41.150
is necessary before
the rest of the device

00:37:41.150 --> 00:37:44.190
or before the work
profile is decrypted.

00:37:44.190 --> 00:37:46.010
So for example,
the common use case

00:37:46.010 --> 00:37:47.780
here is that your
alarm will still

00:37:47.780 --> 00:37:53.340
go off if your device is
rebooted during the night.

00:37:57.660 --> 00:38:01.142
Or that DPCs can be
encryption aware as well,

00:38:01.142 --> 00:38:03.350
and I'll talk a little bit
about some of the features

00:38:03.350 --> 00:38:05.370
that that enables shortly.

00:38:05.370 --> 00:38:07.770
So two headline items
that we've done here

00:38:07.770 --> 00:38:09.770
and we've invested in,
in terms of doubling down

00:38:09.770 --> 00:38:14.330
on the encryption
stack, credentials keys

00:38:14.330 --> 00:38:20.240
can be ejected from memory on
the beck and call of the EMM.

00:38:20.240 --> 00:38:22.760
So there's an API that
you can use, if necessary,

00:38:22.760 --> 00:38:26.300
to eject these keys and have
that data at rest, completely

00:38:26.300 --> 00:38:28.040
secure.

00:38:28.040 --> 00:38:29.660
And likewise with strong auth.

00:38:29.660 --> 00:38:32.540
So there are two types of
different authentication

00:38:32.540 --> 00:38:35.060
that can protect a whole
device or a profile--

00:38:35.060 --> 00:38:39.380
namely pin passwords or pattern.

00:38:39.380 --> 00:38:41.420
And you can enforce
strong policies

00:38:41.420 --> 00:38:42.960
on those types of encryption.

00:38:42.960 --> 00:38:46.130
That's what's called strong
auth, strong authentication.

00:38:46.130 --> 00:38:48.779
There's also other trust
agents plus fingerprints,

00:38:48.779 --> 00:38:50.570
which are slightly less
strong, but they're

00:38:50.570 --> 00:38:52.700
infinitely more convenient.

00:38:52.700 --> 00:38:54.590
What we've done here
is exposed the policy

00:38:54.590 --> 00:38:58.010
so that you can set the timeout
to require this stronger

00:38:58.010 --> 00:39:00.750
form of authentication
as when you need it

00:39:00.750 --> 00:39:03.386
for your deployments.

00:39:03.386 --> 00:39:06.150
OK, so I mentioned about
file-based encryption.

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:10.740
What we've got coming up
here is a feature for FBE.

00:39:10.740 --> 00:39:13.590
This allows for
file-based encryption keys

00:39:13.590 --> 00:39:15.660
to essentially be--
or derived keys,

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:18.090
rather-- to essentially be
escrowed from the device.

00:39:18.090 --> 00:39:20.850
And so, if your user
forgets your password

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:23.700
or if the enterprise portion
of the data on this device

00:39:23.700 --> 00:39:25.680
needs to be recovered,
for whatever reason,

00:39:25.680 --> 00:39:29.850
then you can use your
escrowed keys via your EMM

00:39:29.850 --> 00:39:32.930
to actually reset the
screen lock password

00:39:32.930 --> 00:39:36.980
of that encrypted volume.

00:39:36.980 --> 00:39:40.020
And that's everything in
terms of new features.

00:39:40.020 --> 00:39:43.500
Absent from this talk, this
presentation, was a few demos.

00:39:43.500 --> 00:39:45.120
But don't take my
word for any of it.

00:39:45.120 --> 00:39:48.280
We have the Android
management experience.

00:39:48.280 --> 00:39:49.800
So you can take
any of the things

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:52.320
I've talked to today,
with the exception of some

00:39:52.320 --> 00:39:55.110
of the up and coming stuff
that will be available shortly,

00:39:55.110 --> 00:39:56.220
for a test drive.

00:39:56.220 --> 00:39:58.800
Just go to the URL that
you've got on the screen there

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:00.656
and there's a whole
experience that you

00:40:00.656 --> 00:40:03.030
can get your devices up and
running with these management

00:40:03.030 --> 00:40:06.190
modes and try them
out for yourself.

00:40:06.190 --> 00:40:09.340
So with that, I think we've
got a few more minutes left.

00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:12.840
19 minutes left on
the clock for Q&amp;A.

00:40:12.840 --> 00:40:16.230
So feel free to ask me
anything that's on your minds.

00:40:16.230 --> 00:40:18.030
There's a mic in the
front of the room

00:40:18.030 --> 00:40:19.360
if you'd like to come up.

00:40:19.360 --> 00:40:22.020
Or if you want to shout
out your questions to me,

00:40:22.020 --> 00:40:24.870
I'll try to remember
to repeat them.

00:40:24.870 --> 00:40:26.522
Hey there.

00:40:26.522 --> 00:40:29.104
SPEAKER 1: Just a question
