00:40:29.104 --> 00:40:29.854
that you've shown.

00:40:29.854 --> 00:40:32.234
Are those going to be a mix
of Play services updates?

00:40:32.234 --> 00:40:33.662
OS [INAUDIBLE] updates?

00:40:33.662 --> 00:40:35.049
Or one or the other?

00:40:35.049 --> 00:40:37.590
TOM WATKINS: So the question
was on the future-looking stuff,

00:40:37.590 --> 00:40:39.570
will that be part
of the platform

00:40:39.570 --> 00:40:42.132
or in the services
side of the stack?

00:40:42.132 --> 00:40:43.590
And the answer is
it will be a mix.

00:40:43.590 --> 00:40:47.850
Broadly speaking,
the new capabilities

00:40:47.850 --> 00:40:51.270
for the devices, the new work
profiles on corporate devices,

00:40:51.270 --> 00:40:54.660
that will be platform features
available in an upcoming

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:56.430
version of Android.

00:40:56.430 --> 00:40:59.130
There is a lot that I mentioned
in terms of the security suite,

00:40:59.130 --> 00:41:00.360
Verify Apps capabilities.

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:04.544
That's available in the GMS
Google Play Services stack.

00:41:04.544 --> 00:41:06.210
So that will be
available on all devices

00:41:06.210 --> 00:41:10.790
and be updatable out of band
from a platform release.

00:41:10.790 --> 00:41:13.260
And I don't think I looked
at too many features

00:41:13.260 --> 00:41:15.787
on the Play and the
service side of the stack,

00:41:15.787 --> 00:41:17.370
but there is certainly
a bunch coming.

00:41:17.370 --> 00:41:20.040
I focused more on the
traditional platform stuff.

00:41:20.040 --> 00:41:20.970
So definitely a mix.

00:41:20.970 --> 00:41:22.020
Thank you.

00:41:22.020 --> 00:41:23.123
Hi there.

00:41:23.123 --> 00:41:27.067
SPEAKER 2: [INAUDIBLE]
about the API

00:41:27.067 --> 00:41:30.025
that you mentioned were upcoming
that were checking the security

00:41:30.025 --> 00:41:31.316
[INAUDIBLE] or managed devices.

00:41:31.316 --> 00:41:33.600
Any information you could
give about the future of--

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:34.600
TOM WATKINS: Yeah, sure.

00:41:34.600 --> 00:41:37.210
So another plug for my
colleague, Edward Cunningham.

00:41:37.210 --> 00:41:39.990
His talk is tomorrow
morning, I believe,

00:41:39.990 --> 00:41:42.510
and he'll go into
a lot of detail

00:41:42.510 --> 00:41:45.349
over what we call Safety Net,
which is the suite of security

00:41:45.349 --> 00:41:46.390
services on the platform.

00:41:46.390 --> 00:41:47.348
SPEAKER 2: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:41:47.348 --> 00:41:49.206
Or are there other
things [INAUDIBLE]?

00:41:49.206 --> 00:41:50.830
TOM WATKINS: Well,
safety net comprises

00:41:50.830 --> 00:41:55.150
of Verify Apps and a handful
of other different services

00:41:55.150 --> 00:41:56.030
within it.

00:41:56.030 --> 00:41:58.200
So it's quite a broad category.

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:00.700
Again, from Adrian's talk, we
haven't invested a huge amount

00:42:00.700 --> 00:42:02.540
in the branding of
these security features,

00:42:02.540 --> 00:42:03.873
but it's just called safety net.

00:42:03.873 --> 00:42:05.650
That's kind of what it does.

00:42:05.650 --> 00:42:07.690
Yeah, I'd highly
recommend you look

00:42:07.690 --> 00:42:11.350
at his talk for
details of exact APIs

00:42:11.350 --> 00:42:13.920
that are going to be available
as part of that stack.

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:14.420
Hi.

00:42:14.420 --> 00:42:16.695
SPEAKER 3: Do you
[INAUDIBLE] to iOS devices?

00:42:19.430 --> 00:42:21.020
TOM WATKINS: So the
safety net stack--

00:42:21.020 --> 00:42:22.790
most of our investment
in the Android team

00:42:22.790 --> 00:42:28.320
certainly goes into the
Android side of the platform.

00:42:28.320 --> 00:42:30.010
So the question, just
to repeat it here,

00:42:30.010 --> 00:42:32.360
was can we expand
to iOS devices?

00:42:32.360 --> 00:42:35.692
SPEAKER 3: Not safety net, but
the [INAUDIBLE] management of

00:42:35.692 --> 00:42:39.030
[INAUDIBLE]

00:42:39.030 --> 00:42:41.040
TOM WATKINS: This
is not something

00:42:41.040 --> 00:42:45.090
that we're considering bringing
to iOS or other platforms

00:42:45.090 --> 00:42:47.730
at this point, but we're
really excited about what

00:42:47.730 --> 00:42:51.160
we are able to do on the
Android platform as a whole

00:42:51.160 --> 00:42:53.970
and some of the use
cases that that enables.

00:42:53.970 --> 00:43:00.648
SPEAKER 3: [INAUDIBLE] MDM
for iOS [INAUDIBLE] platform

00:43:00.648 --> 00:43:02.090
for Android.

00:43:02.090 --> 00:43:03.390
TOM WATKINS: Yeah, absolutely.

00:43:03.390 --> 00:43:06.590
So I think our focus is
really on making Android

00:43:06.590 --> 00:43:09.080
as usable and as
manageable as possible

00:43:09.080 --> 00:43:10.790
for any different deployments.

00:43:10.790 --> 00:43:14.060
And again, this is a very broad
spectrum in the Android world.

00:43:14.060 --> 00:43:17.800
So our focus is definitely
on the Android side,

00:43:17.800 --> 00:43:21.560
but I think we find that
features like the work profile

00:43:21.560 --> 00:43:23.930
and, let's say, more
opinionated stances

00:43:23.930 --> 00:43:27.170
that we've taken on how
device management should work,

00:43:27.170 --> 00:43:30.390
we're quite proud of that
ability, that separation.

00:43:30.390 --> 00:43:33.350
And so, we find that in some
cases and some deployments,

00:43:33.350 --> 00:43:37.610
it makes Android
stand out when you're

00:43:37.610 --> 00:43:39.310
looking to choose a platform.

00:43:39.310 --> 00:43:42.494
But absolutely, and when
you're talking BYOD,

00:43:42.494 --> 00:43:43.910
you've got to cater
for any device

00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:46.280
that your users will bring.

00:43:46.280 --> 00:43:48.050
So definitely
something that will

00:43:48.050 --> 00:43:52.230
be top of mind for an IT admin
with your kind of deployment.

00:43:52.230 --> 00:43:52.730
Thank you.

00:43:52.730 --> 00:43:53.790
Any more questions?

00:43:53.790 --> 00:43:55.818
I'll go over here.

00:43:55.818 --> 00:43:56.318
Hi.

00:43:56.318 --> 00:44:07.927
SPEAKER 4: [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:07.927 --> 00:44:10.510
TOM WATKINS: So the question is
about certificate provisioning

00:44:10.510 --> 00:44:11.540
as part of the setup process.

00:44:11.540 --> 00:44:13.840
If you have a certificate
that you need to provision

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:16.510
on your device, how does that
work with profile separation,

00:44:16.510 --> 00:44:17.920
for example?

00:44:17.920 --> 00:44:19.990
And the answer is,
really, to a large extent,

00:44:19.990 --> 00:44:22.850
you can treat the work
profile as a whole device.

00:44:22.850 --> 00:44:24.550
And so there is a
separate key store

00:44:24.550 --> 00:44:27.130
inside the work profile,
separately encrypted

00:44:27.130 --> 00:44:29.170
with the file-based encryption.

00:44:29.170 --> 00:44:31.960
And so, as part of that setup
process for just the work

00:44:31.960 --> 00:44:33.940
profile, you can
have that certificate

00:44:33.940 --> 00:44:36.670
provisioned into the work
profile side of the key store.

00:44:36.670 --> 00:44:39.400
Likewise, we have a
VPN that's available,

00:44:39.400 --> 00:44:42.850
just confined to the
profile or to specific apps

00:44:42.850 --> 00:44:44.342
within the profile.

00:44:44.342 --> 00:44:45.496
Is that helpful?

00:44:45.496 --> 00:44:46.191
You're welcome.

00:44:46.191 --> 00:44:46.690
Hi, there.

00:44:46.690 --> 00:44:47.140
The back.

00:44:47.140 --> 00:44:48.681
SPEAKER 5: Is there
a way to remotely

00:44:48.681 --> 00:44:54.042
opt into a privacy statement
and to the terms of service?

00:44:54.042 --> 00:44:56.015
[INAUDIBLE] EMM.

00:44:56.015 --> 00:44:57.140
TOM WATKINS: Good question.

00:44:57.140 --> 00:45:00.010
So the question is
can you remotely opt

00:45:00.010 --> 00:45:03.220
into the privacy statements
when setting up a device?

00:45:05.830 --> 00:45:08.230
The broad answer is no,
that's not something

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:10.070
that's possible today.

00:45:10.070 --> 00:45:12.250
Particularly on
the BYOD side, we

00:45:12.250 --> 00:45:16.900
want to make sure that end
users actually see this.

00:45:16.900 --> 00:45:20.514
For what I mentioned earlier,
the upcoming feature, the work

00:45:20.514 --> 00:45:22.680
profile that's going to be
available on corp managed

00:45:22.680 --> 00:45:26.080
devices, we found that there
is certainly a strong desire

00:45:26.080 --> 00:45:30.730
to have that set-up
pre-staged and executed

00:45:30.730 --> 00:45:33.280
without an end user going
through it, necessarily.

00:45:33.280 --> 00:45:35.650
So you can set up a work
profile silently just

00:45:35.650 --> 00:45:38.380
on those corp-managed devices.

00:45:38.380 --> 00:45:41.455
That's the feature that's
available on personal devices.

00:45:41.455 --> 00:45:44.800
The end user is supposed to
be going through that flow.

00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:49.270
Likewise, with initially setting
up the corp-managed device,

00:45:49.270 --> 00:45:53.350
you will be required to see
one screen that gets accepted.

00:45:53.350 --> 00:45:57.160
But there's a bunch of ways
that can be made very easy

00:45:57.160 --> 00:45:58.370
and a lightweight process.

00:45:58.370 --> 00:46:01.030
So you can NFC bump these
devices to set them up.

00:46:01.030 --> 00:46:03.430
You can scan a QR code.

00:46:03.430 --> 00:46:04.990
There's a bunch
of different ways

00:46:04.990 --> 00:46:08.850
that you can set up
the corporate side.

00:46:08.850 --> 00:46:10.120
But you will be tapping one--

00:46:10.120 --> 00:46:11.650
SPEAKER 5: The end
user will [INAUDIBLE]

00:46:11.650 --> 00:46:13.990
TOM WATKINS: You'll see one
screen, yes, for that flow.

00:46:13.990 --> 00:46:14.990
Yeah.

00:46:14.990 --> 00:46:15.990
Hi, there.

00:46:15.990 --> 00:46:19.490
SPEAKER 6: Can you talk about if
any features for a [INAUDIBLE]

00:46:19.490 --> 00:46:23.490
user where a device is shared
by [INAUDIBLE] employees,

00:46:23.490 --> 00:46:28.490
and you can log in and
[INAUDIBLE] different

00:46:28.490 --> 00:46:29.490
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:46:32.487 --> 00:46:35.070
TOM WATKINS: So the question is
how do you cater for, I guess,

00:46:35.070 --> 00:46:39.480
multiple user deployments
within a managed device sharing

00:46:39.480 --> 00:46:41.460
the same Android device?

00:46:41.460 --> 00:46:43.920
Now, I will say that, actually,
the strength of the Android

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:48.290
platform and the areas
that we invest most in

00:46:48.290 --> 00:46:51.540
are single user use cases.

00:46:51.540 --> 00:46:54.870
But with that said, there
are a certain number

00:46:54.870 --> 00:46:58.200
of APIs on our device owner,
our corporate-owned API

00:46:58.200 --> 00:47:01.330
surface that will help with
those kind of use cases.

00:47:01.330 --> 00:47:02.850
So one in particular
is, I believe,

00:47:02.850 --> 00:47:05.400
called Create and
Manage User, which

00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:09.060
is part of the Device
Policy Manager class.

00:47:09.060 --> 00:47:12.100
And that will enable certain
aspects of your deployment,

00:47:12.100 --> 00:47:14.160
but I wouldn't say
that you're necessarily

00:47:14.160 --> 00:47:17.100
going to have a full end to end
implementation possible, right

00:47:17.100 --> 00:47:18.840
now, as of today.

00:47:18.840 --> 00:47:21.450
But by all means, take a look
at the APIs that are available,

00:47:21.450 --> 00:47:24.360
and the capability that's
there, and figure out

00:47:24.360 --> 00:47:26.280
if it's sufficient
for your use cases.

00:47:26.280 --> 00:47:27.956
SPEAKER 6: So
there's no way to tie

00:47:27.956 --> 00:47:34.695
into like an operating system
user [INAUDIBLE] user account?

00:47:34.695 --> 00:47:36.070
TOM WATKINS: So
the question was,

00:47:36.070 --> 00:47:37.900
there's no way of tying
into multiple uses

00:47:37.900 --> 00:47:39.700
on the operating
system back to--

00:47:39.700 --> 00:47:43.450
this is more about an
identity kind of problem

00:47:43.450 --> 00:47:45.160
with your deployment,
so you want

00:47:45.160 --> 00:47:47.692
to have a particular
account associated with--

00:47:47.692 --> 00:47:49.442
SPEAKER 6: [INAUDIBLE]
enterprise identity

00:47:49.442 --> 00:47:53.861
and then go back and
[INAUDIBLE] sign into my bank

00:47:53.861 --> 00:47:56.159
and I see the apps or
SalesForce [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:56.159 --> 00:47:56.950
TOM WATKINS: I see.

00:47:56.950 --> 00:48:00.800
OK, this is more of like
an SSO kind of question.

00:48:00.800 --> 00:48:03.130
So yeah, depending on who
your identity provider is

00:48:03.130 --> 00:48:05.800
and what type of accounts
you're talking about,

00:48:05.800 --> 00:48:07.600
there are definitely
tools and features

00:48:07.600 --> 00:48:11.070
available in the platform
that will support that.

00:48:11.070 --> 00:48:13.577
What you might want
to look at is--

00:48:13.577 --> 00:48:14.410
I forget the exact--

00:48:14.410 --> 00:48:19.840
I think it's called Native Apps
SSO, which is a open standard

00:48:19.840 --> 00:48:21.820
and we've shipped an
open library that's

00:48:21.820 --> 00:48:24.510
available for Android and iOS.

00:48:24.510 --> 00:48:26.830
Apple, yes, thank you.

00:48:26.830 --> 00:48:29.920
And so, that's
available to log users

00:48:29.920 --> 00:48:32.770
into multiple different IDPs,
multiple different backends,

00:48:32.770 --> 00:48:37.150
and provide a common
standard at the app level

00:48:37.150 --> 00:48:40.940
to get users signed
in seamlessly.

00:48:40.940 --> 00:48:42.520
And that can be
configured, likewise,

00:48:42.520 --> 00:48:45.190
via NCM, our
configuration management.

00:48:45.190 --> 00:48:47.170
You can point it to
your IDP, give it

00:48:47.170 --> 00:48:49.450
primers for your environments
and your tenant IDs,

00:48:49.450 --> 00:48:50.500
for example.

00:48:50.500 --> 00:48:54.010
So that should help with
these kind of deployments.

00:48:54.010 --> 00:48:55.590
Does that answer your question?

00:48:55.590 --> 00:48:58.060
Thank you.

00:48:58.060 --> 00:49:01.271
Anything else in
terms of questions?

00:49:01.271 --> 00:49:01.770
OK.

00:49:01.770 --> 00:49:03.645
Well, thank you very
much for the time, guys.

00:49:03.645 --> 00:49:05.150
